1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,984
Tonight I'm going to talk to you about that remarkable continent Antarctica - remote, hostile

2
00:00:05,984 --> 00:00:10,020
and at present uninhabited on a permanent basis.

3
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:15,134
For early explorers, it was the ultimate survival contest;

4
00:00:15,134 --> 00:00:20,127
for researchers like me, it remains a place of great intellectual challenge;

5
00:00:20,127 --> 00:00:25,000
while for the modern tourist, it's simply a wilderness of great beauty.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
First, some facts and figures.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,880
Antarctica is a place of extremes-the highest, coldest and windiest continent

8
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:38,290
and over fifty-eight times the size of the UK.

9
00:00:38,290 --> 00:00:43,580
The ice-cap contains almost 70% of the world's fresh water

10
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:45,785
and 90% of its ice,

11
00:00:45,785 --> 00:00:52,197
but with very low snowfall, most of the continent technically falls unbelievably into the

12
00:00:52,197 --> 00:00:53,700
category of 'desert'!

13
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:57,691
Huge icebergs break off the continent each year,

14
00:00:57,691 --> 00:01:01,602
while in winter half the surrounding ocean freezes over,

15
00:01:01,602 --> 00:01:04,550
which means its size almost doubles.

16
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:10,869
Research and exploration has been going on in Antarctica for more than two hundred

17
00:01:10,869 --> 00:01:11,251
years,

18
00:01:11,251 --> 00:01:14,782
and has involved scientists from many different countries,

19
00:01:14,782 --> 00:01:17,530
who work together on research stations.

20
00:01:17,530 --> 00:01:26,445
Here science and technical support have been integrated in a very cost-effective way-our Antarctic

21
00:01:26,445 --> 00:01:32,254
research programme has several summers-only stations and two all-year-round ones;

22
00:01:32,254 --> 00:01:35,660
I was based on one of the all-year-round ones.

23
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:42,400
The research stations are really self-contained communities of about twenty people.

24
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:49,122
There's living and working space, a kitchen with a huge food store, a small hospital

25
00:01:49,122 --> 00:01:54,580
and a well-equipped gym to ensure everyone keeps fit in their spare time.

26
00:01:54,580 --> 00:02:01,116
The station generates its own electricity and communicates with the outside world using a

27
00:02:01,116 --> 00:02:01,940
satellite link.

28
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:07,290
Our station-Zero One-had some special features.

29
00:02:07,290 --> 00:02:13,310
It wasn't built on land but on an ice-shelf, hundreds of metres thick.

30
00:02:13,310 --> 00:02:19,605
Supplies were brought to us on large sledges from a ship fifteen kilometres away

31
00:02:19,605 --> 00:02:20,470
at the ice edge.

32
00:02:20,470 --> 00:02:25,800
Living in the Antarctic hasn't always been so comfortable.

33
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:32,240
Snow build-ups caused enormous problems for four previous stations on the same site,

34
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:35,530
which were buried and finally crushed by the weight.

35
00:02:35,530 --> 00:02:38,274
Fortunately no-one was hurt,

36
00:02:38,274 --> 00:02:43,961
but these buildings became a huge challenge to architects who finally came up with

37
00:02:43,961 --> 00:02:50,330
a remarkable solution -the buildings are placed on platforms which can be raised above

38
00:02:50,330 --> 00:02:58,000
the changing snow level on legs which are extendable.

39
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,160
Food is one of the most important aspects of survival in a polar climate.

40
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:11,009
People living there need to obtain a lot more energy from their food, both to keep warm

41
00:03:11,009 --> 00:03:13,540
and to undertake heavy physical work.

42
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:21,260
Maybe you know that an adult in the UK will probably need about 1,700

43
00:03:21,260 --> 00:03:23,887
kilocalories a day on average;

44
00:03:23,887 --> 00:03:29,000
someone in Antarctica will need about 3,500-just over double!

45
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:35,470
This energy is provided by foods which are high in carbohydrate and fat.

46
00:03:35,470 --> 00:03:41,120
Rations for fieldwork present an additional problem.

47
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:44,333
They need to provide maximum energy,

48
00:03:44,333 --> 00:03:48,690
but they must also be compact and light for easy transport.

49
00:03:48,690 --> 00:03:56,160
Special boxes are prepared, each containing enough food for one person for twenty days.

50
00:03:56,160 --> 00:04:01,213
You may be familiar with coffee processed by freeze-drying,

51
00:04:01,213 --> 00:04:06,146
which preserves the quality of the food product while making a large saving in

52
00:04:06,146 --> 00:04:11,740
weight-well, this type of presentation is ideal in our situation.

53
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:18,708
It wasn't available to earlier polar explorers, whose diet was commonly insufficient for their

54
00:04:18,708 --> 00:04:19,090
health.

55
00:04:19,090 --> 00:04:24,574
I think that being at the cutting edge of science has a special appeal

56
00:04:24,574 --> 00:04:28,110
for everyone working in Antarctica, in whatever capacity.

57
00:04:28,110 --> 00:04:33,028
As a marine biologist, my own research was fascinating;

58
00:04:33,028 --> 00:04:39,070
but it's perhaps climate change research that is the most crucial field of study.

59
00:04:39,070 --> 00:04:47,127
Within this general field, surveying changes in the volume and stability of the ice-cap

60
00:04:47,127 --> 00:04:51,997
is vital, since these may have profound effects on world sea levels

61
00:04:51,997 --> 00:04:53,560
and on ocean currents.

62
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:59,575
A second important area is monitoring the size of the hole in the ozone

63
00:04:59,575 --> 00:05:05,750
layer above Antarctica, since this is an indicator of global ultra-violet radiation levels.

64
00:05:05,750 --> 00:05:12,019
Thirdly, bubbles in the ice-sheet itself provide an index of pollution

65
00:05:12,019 --> 00:05:19,210
because frozen inside them are samples of previous atmospheres over the past 500,000

66
00:05:19,210 --> 00:05:20,332
years,

67
00:05:20,332 --> 00:05:25,319
and these provide us with evidence for the effects of such human activities as agriculture

68
00:05:25,319 --> 00:05:26,000
and industry.

69
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:33,294
There are an increasing number of opportunities for young people to work for a

70
00:05:33,294 --> 00:05:38,725
period in Antarctica-not only as research assistants in projects like mine,

71
00:05:38,725 --> 00:05:45,338
but also in a wide range of junior administrative and technical positions including vacancies

72
00:05:45,338 --> 00:05:46,300
for map-makers.

73
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:52,386
I hope that the insights I've provided will encourage you to take up these

74
00:05:52,386 --> 00:05:55,500
opportunities in this fascinating continent.
